<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
A single attribute set literal defines the same top-level key more than once.
In Nix, duplicate keys are rejected when the attrset is evaluated, so the
expression fails at runtime.
</p>
<p>
This often happens after copy/paste or refactoring, especially in large
attribute sets such as derivations.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Keep each top-level key unique within an attribute set literal. Rename one
binding, merge the values deliberately, or move nested data under distinct
attribute paths.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Problematic:</p>
<sample language="nix">
let
  badAttrset = {
    pname = "demo";
    pname = "other";
  };
in
null
</sample>
<p>Correct:</p>
<sample language="nix">
let
  goodAttrset = {
    pname = "demo";
    version = "1.0";
  };
in
null
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>
Nix Reference Manual:
<a href="https://nix.dev/manual/nix/latest/language/syntax#attrs-literal">
Attribute Set</a>
</li>
<li>
CWE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/710.html">CWE-710:
Improper Adherence to Coding Standards</a>
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
